WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.140
From seeming so unimportant that she was written
out of the comics to proving herself worthy

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.769
to carry the title of the Mighty Thor - and
plenty of comics-worthy twists and turns over

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:13.260
the decades in between - here's the truth
behind Jane Foster.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.400
Jane Foster was introduced in Journey Into
Mystery #84, only a month after Thor himself

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.570
made his first appearance in Marvel Comics.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.960
While issue #83 had been devoted to introducing
the world to Dr. Donald Blake and his thundering

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.800
alter ego, the following issue took a little
time to fill out Thor's supporting cast, and

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.300
it's not tough to figure out where creators
Stan Lee and Jack Kirby drew their inspiration.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.320
If Thor was Marvel's take on Superman, then
Jane Foster was Lee and Kirby's riff on Lois

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:41.820
Lane.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.320
Like her DC counterpart, she was the coworker
that the hero was pining for, who was far

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.100
more interested in the superhero than the
secret identity.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.740
The only big difference was that Jane was
a nurse working with Blake rather than a reporter,

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.880
and at least in her first few appearances,
Jane was a redhead rather than a brunette.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:02.380
But over the next few years, Jane would distinguish
herself from Lois by learning Thor's secret

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.440
identity and being brought into the mythologically
inspired world of Asgard.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.680
For a while, at least.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:10.240
"You're…"

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:11.440
"I'm Loki.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.360
You may have…"

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.420
"That was for New York."

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.200
"I like her!"

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.360
Thor and Jane hoped to marry, but if you've
read enough comics from the era, you know

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.050
something had to get in the way.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.281
The major obstacle for this set of lovebirds
came in the form of Thor's father, Odin, who

00:01:26.281 --> 00:01:30.740
forbade the marriage on the grounds that Thor
was an immortal Asgardian thunder god, and

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:31.240
Jane was human.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.420
There's a pretty simple solution that problem,
though: make Jane a goddess.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:41.900
That's what happens in 1967's Thor #136, where
Thor takes Jane home to Asgard to meet the

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.400
family.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.420
Odin, in a seemingly benevolent move, makes
Jane a goddess, granting her an Asgardian

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.120
costume and the power of flight.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.160
Unfortunately for Jane, this gift comes with
a few strings attached.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:58.160
She has to prove she has "the courage of an
immortal" to stay in Asgard, and Odin decides

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:02.390
that the best way to test Jane's bravery is
to lock her in a dark room with the Unknown,

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:07.300
a Lovecraftian horror that can only be summoned
by a gigantic magic tuning fork.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.540
Jane freaks out upon being confronted with
the Unknown, and declares her desire to get

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:12.799
the heck out of there.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:16.930
With that, Odin declares her unfit, strips
her of her powers, and boots her back to Earth

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.800
without any of her memories of Thor.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.200
She's given a job with hunky doctor Keith
Kincaid, with whom she immediately falls in

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:23.700
love.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.799
Jane made an unexpected return to the pages
of Thor in issue #231 from 1975.

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:34.819
Jane, mortal as ever, is on her quite literal
deathbed, and can only be saved by divine

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.520
intervention.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.159
In this case, the role of the divine is played
by Sif, the goddess who had been romantically

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:44.290
linked to Thor since somewhere around the
11th century, and returned to Thor's life

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.700
while Jane was off romancing Dr. Kincaid.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.760
Sif discovers that Thor still has feelings
for Jane, and when she learns about Jane's

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.330
ailment, she pulls off some eldritch magic
in issue #236, merging her own godly essence

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:00.280
with Jane's mortal form, seemingly sacrificing
herself in the process.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.480
Jane, now recovered and with a taste for adventure
that Thor attributed to Sif's spirit inhabiting

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.909
her body, goes back to hanging out with the
God of Thunder on Asgard, and even gains the

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:14.239
ability to transform into Sif, just as Don
Blake transformed into Thor.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.420
None of these changes lasted all that long,
though.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.700
Eventually, Dr. Kincaid started wondering
where his fiancee had gone, and Sif returned

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.489
to the book as her usual godly self.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:27.360
Marvel's Thor wasn't some modern hero inspired
by the legendary Thunder God.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:31.780
This was literally the Norse god Thor, who
lived in Asgard, had been worshipped by the

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.950
Vikings, fought Frost Giants, commanded lightning,
and had a shape-shifting, troublemaking half-brother

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:37.760
named Loki.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.120
"There's Alfheim.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:40.800
Vanaheim.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:42.700
Jotunheim.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.040
And Asgard.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.700
And that's where I come from."

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:51.360
He just happened to be bound to the very human
Don Blake, who had more earthly concerns and

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.460
taught Thor some lessons about being mortal.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.419
As time went on, the stories gravitated to
more epic, fantastical adventures in the realm

00:03:58.420 --> 00:03:59.629
of gods and giants.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.380
Unlike, say, Peter Parker, whose real-life
concerns balanced out the superheroism of

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.220
Spider-Man, Don Blake felt more and more like
an unnecessary element of Thor comics.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.920
Blake was not, in fact, a real person, but
an identity that Odin created to teach his

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:18.280
son humility, so when Walt Simonson arrived
to write and draw a five-year run that would

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.460
become the definitive take on Thor in 1983,
one of the first things he did was ditch Blake

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.160
entirely, reasoning that he'd outlived his
purpose.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:30.960
The stories refocused on those fantastical,
mythological elements, and Thor even got a

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:35.700
new "secret" identity in the form of Sigurd
Jarlson, a construction worker who was literally

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.460
just Thor wearing glasses.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.680
Don't worry about Blake's friends at his medical
practice, though: in Thor #354, Fandral the

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.260
Dashing dropped by to give his nurses and
staff a bag of Asgardian gold as their severance

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:47.760
pay.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.200
With that, Thor's romantic life was recentered
around Sif.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.380
While Jane did appear late in the Simonson
run, it was only briefly, and revealed that

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.700
she and Keith Kincaid were happily married
and expecting a child.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.260
After a year-long trip to an alternate universe
and back, Marvel's Fantastic Four and Avengers

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.770
books took a "back-to-basics" approach to
their characters in the late 1990s, and the

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:13.040
relaunched Thor was no exception, bringing
back the idea of giving the God of Thunder

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.660
a mortal civilian identity.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:20.000
In this case, Thor took over the body of paramedic
Jake Olson, who just happened to wind up working

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.660
in the same hospital as Dr. Jane Foster.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.050
Thor was initially reluctant to reveal his
true identity to Jane, given her life with

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:31.430
Keith Kincaid and their son Jimmy, but she
eventually figured it out herself when "Jake"

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.740
slipped into the faux-Shakespearean dialect
that Marvel's Asgardians tend to use.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.940
With that, she re-entered Thor's life and
his stories, and floated around the Marvel

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:44.720
Universe for the next few years, working briefly
for Tony Stark as his personal doctor and

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.650
showing up in the aftermath of Civil War as
the resident physician for the underground

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:49.580
Avengers.

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.400
Things weren't exactly great, though.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:55.911
Jane would lose her husband and son to a car
accident, which happened off-panel, and she

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.760
was herself diagnosed with cancer.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.900
The good news is that all of this would pave
the way for her greatest role in the Marvel

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:02.900
Universe yet.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.520
While they would eventually draw inspiration
from more contemporary comics such as Planet

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.680
Hulk and Civil War, the earliest entries in
the Marvel Cinematic Universe drew pretty

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.340
heavily from the first few years of Marvel
Comics.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.660
Iron Man, for instance, brought in Pepper
Potts and Happy Hogan, who hadn't really been

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.640
featured in the comics for some time, and
Avengers brought the team together to face

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.340
down a threat from Loki, just like the originals
did in 1963.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:31.680
2011's Thor was no exception in that regard,
and in addition to the fantastic realms of

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.040
Asgard and Jotunheim, land of the Frost Giants,
it included Jane Foster as Thor's human love

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:37.540
interest.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:42.520
Jane was played by Natalie Portman, and instead
of having a medical job, she was an astrophysicist

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.020
studying cosmic phenomena.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:46.100
You know, like rainbow bridges and stuff.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:46.700
Science.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.260
"Your ancestors called it magic, and you call
it science.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.300
But I come from a place where they are one
in the same thing."

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:59.840
Portman's version of Jane is a key element
in blending the fantastical and sci-fi elements

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.060
that made Thor such a distinct character in
the Marvel Universe.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.980
In the following film, The Dark World, she
took a trip to Asgard herself.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:11.640
Sadly, by the time we caught up with Thor
again in Ragnarok, she had dumped him, probably

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.370
due to Thor's insistence on spending two years
flying around in space and decapitating fire

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.320
giants instead of spending any time on their
relationship.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.780
"Sorry to hear that Jane dumped you."

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.260
"She didn't dump me, you know.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:24.860
I dumped her.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:25.760
It was a mutual dumping."

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:31.280
After the 2014 Marvel event Original Sin,
the Son of Odin believing he was unworthy,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.810
and therefore unable to wield Mjolnir, the
Marvel Universe was Thorless, but only for

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:36.300
a bit.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.640
The Odinson himself was, of course, still
a god, and continued to fight against evil

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.200
wielding a magic axe named Jarnbjorn, but
without his hammer, he wasn't great at it.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.500
His first outing ended with him getting his
arm cut off with his own axe.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:54.340
Fortunately, someone else was around to lift
Mjolnir and become the all-new, all-different

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:54.840
Thor.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:59.200
And since the new Thor also happened to be
a woman, it was actually pretty easy to tell

00:07:59.200 --> 00:07:59.920
them apart.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:04.880
Initially, the new Thor's identity was kept
secret from both the readers and the Odinson.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.970
His first theory was that it was his mother
Frigga, but that turned out to be wrong.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:13.020
Eventually, he discovered that this new Thor
was Jane Foster, who had lifted the hammer

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.840
with the motivation that there should always
be a Thor around to protect people.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:21.780
Her transformation also kept her alive while
her mortal form was, by all appearances, dying

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.640
from cancer.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:27.600
Jane served admirably in the role of Thor,
but eventually she ran into a pretty big problem:

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.860
Mangog, an unstoppable, monstrous creature
formed from the quote, "collected rage, the

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:37.180
unbridled hatred" of a race of billions slaughtered
by Odin in ages past.

00:08:37.509 --> 00:08:41.860
With no other way to defeat him, Jane chained
Mangog to Mjolnir and threw it into the heart

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:46.449
of the sun, destroying both Mangog and the
hammer itself, robbing her of her ability

00:08:46.449 --> 00:08:47.200
to transform.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:52.340
With that, her mortal body died, but she was
soon revived by Odin and his son, now Thor

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:56.540
once again, who channeled the god-storm that
had powered Mjolnir to resurrect her.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:59.700
She then focused on her treatment, and her
cancer went into remission.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.139
Jane recently appeared in War of the Realms,
a massive Marvel crossover in which all of

00:09:05.139 --> 00:09:09.560
Asgard's enemies united under the direction
of the dark elf Malekith the lay siege to

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.720
Earth, dividing it up among themselves.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.480
At the end, she briefly returned to the role
of Thor, wielding a Mjolnir from another dimension

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.029
and fighting alongside three other versions
of Thor from different times.

00:09:20.029 --> 00:09:24.779
It ended with Jane's hammer shattering, and
revealing itself to be Undrjarn the All-Weapon,

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.720
a mystical metal capable of shaping itself
into whatever weapon she needed.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:33.860
Another major consequence of War of the Realms
was the death of the Valkyries, including

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:38.800
Brunhilde, who has been known as superhero
Valkyrie since she debuted in 1973, inspiring

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.880
the MCU character of the same name.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:45.300
When Jane visited Brunhilde in the morgue
to pay her final respects, Brunhilde's spirit

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.400
charged Jane with taking up the mantle of
her responsibility.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:53.140
Jane agreed and took on her latest identity
as Valkyrie and continued her ascendance as

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.089
one of Marvel's most prominent heroines.

00:09:56.199 --> 00:10:00.930
In July 2019, Marvel Studios laid out its
plans for Phase Four of the Marvel Cinematic

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.820
Universe at San Diego Comic-Con 2019.

00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:07.639
There were plenty of welcome announcements
in there, from a Black Widow solo film to

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:12.120
the arrival of a Multiverse of Madness in
the sequel to Doctor Strange, but one of the

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.920
most interesting announcements came when the
next Thor movie was discussed.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:21.300
Thor: Love and Thunder was announced for release
in November 2021, with Taika Waititi returning

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:24.160
to direct after the success of Thor: Ragnarok.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:27.999
Chris Hemsworth will be back as Thor, and
Tessa Thompson will return as Valkyrie, but

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.760
there's going to be another, more surprising
return.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.360
After being absent for Ragnarok and seen only
in recycled footage from The Dark World when

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:40.350
she appeared briefly in Avengers: Endgame,
Natalie Portman is returning to the MCU as

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.960
Jane Foster, and that's not the only news.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:48.000
"It introduces, for the first time, female
Thor."

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.100
Jane will be hefting Mjolnir herself and presumably
fighting alongside the Odinson and his new

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:57.980
axe, Stormbreaker.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.649
That will make Jane only the fourth person
in the MCU to lift the hammer, and considering

00:11:02.649 --> 00:11:07.600
that the other three are Thor, Odin, and Captain
America, that's very good company to be in.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.420
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.760
Plus, even more Looper videos about the Marvel
Comics are coming soon.

